            "notes": [
                {
                    "__type__": "Note",
                    "fields": [
                        "<div>&nbsp;Name the performance specification for first-order systems.</div>",
                        "Time constant",
                        ""
                    ],
                    "guid": "pUG95z!/~I",
                    "note_model_uuid": "3a9fd11a-cd92-11f1-9564-7dad70bd0806",
                    "tags": []
                },
                {
                    "__type__": "Note",
                    "fields": [
                        "<div>&nbsp;What does the performance specification for a first-order system tell\nus?</div>",
                        "The time for the step response to reach 67% of its final value",
                        ""
                    ],
                    "guid": "h_PgGh}aJM",
                    "note_model_uuid": "3a9fd11a-cd92-11f1-9564-7dad70bd0806",
                    "tags": []
                },
                {
                    "__type__": "Note",
                    "fields": [
                        "<div>In a system with an input and an output, what poles generate the\nsteady-state response?</div>",
                        "The input pole",
                        ""
                    ],
                    "guid": "qy_OR7[/P8",
                    "note_model_uuid": "3a9fd11a-cd92-11f1-9564-7dad70bd0806",
                    "tags": []
                },
                {
                    "__type__": "Note",
                    "fields": [
                        "<div>In a system with an input and an output, what poles generate the\ntransient response?</div>",
                        "The system poles",
                        ""
                    ],
                    "guid": "wL0SBw1Eq[",
                    "note_model_uuid": "3a9fd11a-cd92-11f1-9564-7dad70bd0806",
                    "tags": []
                },
                {
                    "__type__": "Note",
                    "fields": [
                        "<div>The imaginary part of a pole generates what part of a response?</div>",
                        "The radian frequency of a sinusoidal response",
                        ""
                    ],
                    "guid": "h/hFE4i9,P",
                    "note_model_uuid": "3a9fd11a-cd92-11f1-9564-7dad70bd0806",
                    "tags": []
                },
                {
                    "__type__": "Note",
                    "fields": [
                        "<div>The real part of a pole generates what part of a response?</div>",
                        "The time constant of an exponential response, the exponential decay",
                        ""
                    ],
                    "guid": "RajzjY2uH<",
                    "note_model_uuid": "3a9fd11a-cd92-11f1-9564-7dad70bd0806",
                    "tags": []
                },
                {
                    "__type__": "Note",
                    "fields": [
                        "<div>What is the difference between the natural frequency and the damped\nfrequency of oscillation?</div>",
                        "Natural frequency is the frequency of the system with all damping removed; the damped frequency of&nbsp;oscillation is the frequency of oscillation with damping in the system.",
                        ""
                    ],
                    "guid": "si|>CT!<c^",
                    "note_model_uuid": "3a9fd11a-cd92-11f1-9564-7dad70bd0806",
                    "tags": []
                },
                {
                    "__type__": "Note",
                    "fields": [
                        "<div>If a pole is moved with a constant imaginary part, what will the\nresponses have in common?</div>",
                        "Their damped frequency of oscillation will be the same.",
                        ""
                    ],
                    "guid": "gmt22dEbl@",
                    "note_model_uuid": "3a9fd11a-cd92-11f1-9564-7dad70bd0806",
                    "tags": []
                },
                {
                    "__type__": "Note",
                    "fields": [
                        "<div><a></a>If a pole is moved with a constant real part, what will the responses\nhave in common?</div>",
                        "They will all exist under the same exponential decay envelope.",
                        ""
                    ],
                    "guid": "hi[=I>R]ME",
                    "note_model_uuid": "3a9fd11a-cd92-11f1-9564-7dad70bd0806",
                    "tags": []
                },
                {
                    "__type__": "Note",
                    "fields": [
                        "<div>If a pole is moved along a radial line extending from the origin,\nwhat will the responses have in common?</div>",
                        "They will all have the same percent overshoot although differently scaled in time.",
                        ""
                    ],
                    "guid": "op/(hvI@7a",
                    "note_model_uuid": "3a9fd11a-cd92-11f1-9564-7dad70bd0806",
                    "tags": []
                },
                {
                    "__type__": "Note",
                    "fields": [
                        "<div>List five specifications for a second-order underdamped system.</div>",
                        "\\(\\zeta\\),&nbsp;\\(\\omega_n\\),&nbsp;\\(T_p\\),&nbsp;\\(OS\\),&nbsp;\\(T_s\\)",
                        ""
                    ],
                    "guid": "EkP|N!@eN^",
                    "note_model_uuid": "3a9fd11a-cd92-11f1-9564-7dad70bd0806",
                    "tags": []
                },
                {
                    "__type__": "Note",
                    "fields": [
                        "<div>For Question 11 how many specifications completely determine the\nresponse?</div>",
                        "Only two since a second-order system is completely defined by two component parameters",
                        ""
                    ],
                    "guid": "v#C[`[o.Zs",
                    "note_model_uuid": "3a9fd11a-cd92-11f1-9564-7dad70bd0806",
                    "tags": []
                },
                {
                    "__type__": "Note",
                    "fields": [
                        "<div>What pole locations characterize (1) the underdamped system, (2) the\noverdamped system, and (3) the critically damped system?</div>",
                        "(1) Complex, (2) Real, (3) Multiple real",
                        ""
                    ],
                    "guid": "eF|M+|)aNK",
                    "note_model_uuid": "3a9fd11a-cd92-11f1-9564-7dad70bd0806",
                    "tags": []
                },
                {
                    "__type__": "Note",
                    "fields": [
                        "<div>(1) Name two conditions under which the response generated by a pole can\nbe neglected.</div>",
                        "Pole's real part is large compared to the dominant poles, (2) Pole is near a zero",
                        ""
                    ],
                    "guid": "Q4f7fqtQ,/",
                    "note_model_uuid": "3a9fd11a-cd92-11f1-9564-7dad70bd0806",
                    "tags": []
                },
                {
                    "__type__": "Note",
                    "fields": [
                        "<div>How can you justify pole-zero cancellation?</div>",
                        "If the residue at that pole is much smaller than the residues at other poles",
                        ""
                    ],
                    "guid": "BR?.`8CH*U",
                    "note_model_uuid": "3a9fd11a-cd92-11f1-9564-7dad70bd0806",
                    "tags": []
                },
                {
                    "__type__": "Note",
                    "fields": [
                        "<div>Does the solution of the state equation yield the output response of\nthe system?</div>",
                        "No; one must then use the output equation",
                        ""
                    ],
                    "guid": "u)jr*>aaF}",
                    "note_model_uuid": "3a9fd11a-cd92-11f1-9564-7dad70bd0806",
                    "tags": []
                },
                {
                    "__type__": "Note",
                    "fields": [
                        "What is the relationship between&nbsp;\\((s[I] - [A])^{-1}\\)&nbsp;which appeared during the Laplace transformation solution of the state equations, and the state-transition matrix, which appeared during the classical solution of the state equation?",
                        "The Laplace transform of the state transition matrix is&nbsp;\\((s[I] - [A])^{-1}\\)",
                        ""
                    ],
                    "guid": "PIahldzrAk",
                    "note_model_uuid": "3a9fd11a-cd92-11f1-9564-7dad70bd0806",
                    "tags": []
                },
                {
                    "__type__": "Note",
                    "fields": [
                        "<div>Name a major advantage of using time-domain techniques for the\nsolution of the response.</div>",
                        "Computer simulation",
